  "verbalization": "Nifedipine, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, is directly linked to Cyp3a7-cyp3a51p through its inclusion in the class of dihydropyridines and its binding to the same target. This relationship is supported by multiple paths, all of which converge on the mechanism of action involving the inhibition of Cyp3a7-cyp3a51p. The evidence suggests that Nifedipine, through its classification as a dihydropyridine, interacts with Cyp3a7-cyp3a51p, which is involved in drug metabolism. The presence of a lowest common ancestor (LCA) between Nifedipine and Nisoldipine, both of which bind to Cyp3a7-cyp3a51p, further strengthens this connection. The observed scores in the paths indicate a consistent and plausible mechanistic link, with no conflicting evidence presented. The relationship is biologically plausible, as Cyp3a7-cyp3a51p is a key enzyme in the metabolism of many drugs, and the interaction between Nifedipine and this enzyme is well-documented in the context of drug-target interactions.",
